// TODO(pending): enable over-limit test once the corpus runner supports "Expected runtime failure".
//
// ── What this case tests ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
//
// `compactarray·new` caps its body packing buffer at 8192 bytes (`CONTAINER_NEW_BODY_CAPACITY` in
// `vmbin/handlers/container.c`). **Exactly 1024 `int64` elements must succeed** (covered by
// `array/body_capacity_exact.kv`), while **1025 elements must fail with `0x4301`**
// (`RUNTIMEC_CONTAINER_DIAG_NEW_BODY_EXCEEDS_BUFFER`) without truncation or silent fallback.
//
// ── Why this cannot run in the active corpus yet ─────────────────────────────
//
// The corpus runner currently supports pass / fail / blocked plus `// Expected error: <substring>`
// for layout errors, **without an "Expected runtime failure" state**.
//
// Placing a 1025-element case in the active corpus would record a **fail** even though the runtime
// rightly rejected the over-limit input. Proper rejections must stay distinguishable from regressions.
//
// ── Do not substitute proxy assertions ───────────────────────────────────────
//
// Do **not** replace this with a static check asserting `CONTAINER_NEW_BODY_CAPACITY == 8192`:
// changing the limit must flip the behavioral test outcome, which a literal check does not verify.
//
// ── Why this file lives under `known-limitations/` ───────────────────────────
//
// `known-limitations/` is excluded from runner discovery so it does not produce false reds.
// Move this case to `array/` once `// Expected runtime failure: 0x4301` is supported.
//
// Related staged cases: `pointer_parameter.kv`, `address_and_deref.kv`, `structref_parameter.kv`.
//
// ── Intended form once promoted ──────────────────────────────────────────────
//
//   // Expected runtime failure: 0x4301
//   rwfunc test() -> () {
//       vals = [<1025 int64 elements>]
//       println(compactarray·numel(vals))
//   }
//
// Kept as a comment-only placeholder below (omitting the 1025-element literal).

